  "citations": [
    {
      "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
      "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/3#1~1",
      "quote": "However, the mere presence within a trade mark of a person's name, a descriptive term, a geographical place name or a reference to the size of packages or intended use of a product does not automatically demonstrate a connotation sufficient to trigger section 43."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
      "quote": "Qualifiers (e.g. “made in”, “product of”) are not necessary for a connotation to be present in a mark. A geographical reference used without any qualifying information may not result in consumers being deceived, but may be likely to cause confusion. A trade mark containing a standalone geographical reference will cause confusion if each of the following criteria are met: Consumers would have reason to associate the goods/services with the geographical location; The association between the place and the goods/services would influence the purchasing decision of an average consumer of the goods/services. There are no other elements in the mark, or surrounding circumstances, which would mitigate the risk of confusion."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
      "quote": "Surrounding circumstances include the manner in which the goods/services are purchased, the physical characteristics of goods and any other factor which would affect consumer perceptions."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/9#1",
      "quote": "If a trade mark containing a geographical reference is found to connote geographical origin, consideration must then be given to whether ordinary consumers would be deceived or confused as a result. This includes assessing whether the scope of the specification would include goods/services produced in areas outside the geographical location when a consumer would reasonably expect them to originate from the location referenced in the trade mark."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/1~1",
      "quote": "There must be a real and obvious danger of the buying public being deceived and/or confused by the secondary meaning within the trade mark. A possibility of confusion or a suggestion that deception might occur is not sufficient."
    },
    {
      "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/1~1",
      "quote": "It is enough if the ordinary person entertains a reasonable doubt."
    }
  ],
  "answer": "A place name does not automatically cause confusion: the question is what it connotes for the goods or services and how consumers would understand it. The Manual expressly says that a geographical place name alone does not automatically establish a sufficient connotation. [TMM/Part29/3#1~1]\n\n**Act — legal ground:** Section 43 requires rejection where, because of a connotation of the mark or a sign within it, use in relation to the particular goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion. [TMA1995/s43]\n\n**Manual practice — standalone place names:** The Manual says confusion arises where all three criteria are met:\n1. Consumers have reason to associate the goods or services with the place.\n2. That association would influence the average consumer’s purchasing decision.\n3. No other elements of the mark or surrounding circumstances mitigate the risk.\nWords such as “made in” or “product of” are not necessary. [TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1]\n\n**Manual practice — contextual assessment:** Consider how the goods or services are purchased, their physical characteristics and other factors affecting consumer perceptions. Where the place name connotes geographical origin, assess whether the specification includes goods or services produced elsewhere despite consumers reasonably expecting origin in the named place. [TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1; TMM/Part29/9#1]\n\n**Manual practice — threshold:** A mere possibility is insufficient: there must be a real and obvious danger of confusion from the secondary meaning. The Manual’s quoted judicial guidance says it is enough if the ordinary person entertains a reasonable doubt. [TMM/Part29/3/3/1~1]"
}
